%I A400013 #21 Nov 27 2026 16:55:08
%S A400013 872254719649,872254719689,872254719691,872254719697,872254719733,
%T A400013 872254719751,872254719767,872254719779,872254719823,872254719857,
%U A400013 872254719859,872254719863,872254719871,872254719923,872254719929,872254719953,872254719977,872254719989,872254720039
%N A400013 Primes p for which pi_{120;61,109}(p) - pi_{120;1,49}(p) = -1, where pi_{m;a,b,...}(x) is the number of primes <= x which are congruent to a or b or ... (mod m).
%C A400013 Let H be a proper subgroup of (Z/NZ)* containing all the squares, and S != H be a coset of H. Assuming the generalized Riemann hypothesis (GRH) and the Grand Simplicity Hypothesis (GSH; called LI in page 29 of Greg Martin's 2010 link), then pi_{N;S}(x) > pi_{N;H}(x) occurs more often than not: the set {pi_{N;S}(x) > pi_{N;H}(x)} has logarithmic density greater than 1/2. This phenomenon is called "Chebyshev's bias".
%C A400013 Chebyshev's bias can also be seen from the perspective of Dirichlet characters. Let T be a nontrivial coset of a nontrivial subgroup of real Dirichlet characters modulo N. Let H be the subgroup of (Z/NZ)* of elements r such that chi(r) = 1 for all chi in T, and S be the coset of H consisting of elements r such that chi(r) = -1 for all chi in T, then pi_{N;S}(x) > pi_{N;H}(x) occurs more often than not.
%C A400013 Here we have N = 120, H = {1,49} and S = {61,109}, corresponding to T = {chi_8, chi_{-8}, chi_24, chi_{-24}, chi_40, chi_{-40}, chi_120, chi_{-120}}, where chi_d = (d/.) is the Kronecker symbol viewed as a character modulo N = 120.
%C A400013 Assuming GRH and LI, the set of x with pi_{120;61,109}(x) > pi_{120;1,49}(x) has logarithmic density 0.99866, making the race between {1,49} and {61,109} the second least biased among the 15 races between cosets of index 16 in (Z/120Z)*; see the Zenodo preprint "Logarithmic densities of prime number races between the cosets of subgroups containing the squares modulo 120" below. Along with the least biased index-16 race between {1,49} and {73,97} (A400012), these are the only two races with known sign-changing zones below x0 = 7*10^12.
%C A400013 Let D(x) = pi_{120;61,109}(x) - pi_{120;1,49}(x). (We have D(x0) = 80282; for the concrete numbers of primes below x0 in each congruence class modulo 120, see the file "pi_mod120_at_7e12.csv" in the Zenodo preprint). There is only one sign-changing zone giving a total of 121599 terms below x0:
%C A400013   a(1..121599) = 872254719649-874745710417 (there are 50645523 primes a(1) <= p <= a(121599) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -1014 at p = 873594968929).
%H A400013 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="/A400013/b400013.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A400013 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24516487">Logarithmic densities of prime number races between the cosets of subgroups containing the squares modulo 120</a>, preprint, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A400013 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="/A400013/a400013.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..121599</a> (all terms below 7*10^12).
%H A400013 Greg Martin and Daniel Fiorilli, <a href="https://personal.math.ubc.ca/~gerg/slides/Chennai-31Aug10.pdf">Prime number races: An asymptotic formula for the densities</a>, Analytic and Combinatorial Number Theory, Institute of Mathematical Sciences, Chennai, India. August 31, 2010.
%H A400013 Greg Martin, <a href="https://personal.math.ubc.ca/~gerg/slides/Canberra-29Nov18.pdf">Prime number races</a>, Pure Math Seminar, UNSW Canberra. November 29, 2018.
%H A400013 Andrey S. Shchebetov, <a href="https://math101.guru/wp-content/uploads/2018/09/01-A3-Presentation-v7.3EN-no.pdf">Testing Chebyshev's bias for prime numbers up to 10^15</a>, EUCYS 2018.
%H A400013 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chebyshev%27s_bias">Chebyshev's bias</a>.
%o A400013 (PARI) my(k=0); forprime(p=872254719649, 9e11, if(p%120==1||p%120==49, k--); if(p%120==61||p%120==109, k++); if(k==-1, print1(p, ", ")))
%Y A400013 Cf. A400012, A402745, A402746.
%Y A400013 See A401958 for races between cosets of index 4 in (Z/24Z)*.
